  "narrative": "No due assessments or validation are required, and the voluntary validation budget is exhausted. The best current priorities are (a) clarifying two unresolved MDM2 subgroup effects and (b) testing whether accepted dependencies are modified by biologically plausible biomarkers rather than merely stable across strata. The two refinements to H100 and H170 add a screen-quality threshold of screen_roc_auc >= 0.93. If those signed estimates are positive after the registered direction and remain meaningful relative to the MDM2 score scale, they indicate that the earlier TP53-loss or MYC-amplification attenuation may have been caused by lower-quality screens. If the intervals cross zero, this supports true attenuation in those genetic contexts rather than a screening artifact. The six interaction proposals formalize effect modification. For USP7, MYC expression may alter p53-pathway dependency because MYC coactivation of transcription, stress response, and USP7/TP53 biology can change cellular tolerance to USP7 loss. For NFE2L2, high EMT may modify KEAP1 dependency because oxidative stress handling differs across cell-state programs. For SMARCA2, ASCL2 expression tests a lineage/transcriptional-state modifier of chromatin-remodeler dependency. For WRN, high SLFN11 is a plausible replication-stress/DNA-damage-response marker that may change the relative importance of helicase dependency in MSI-high lines. For STAT3, PIK3CA mutation provides a direct pathway context for PTEN-loss effect modification. For MDM2, the screen-quality interaction directly tests whether the smaller, unresolved low-ROC effect reflects effect modification by assay quality. These remain dataset-specific associations; interactions should only change conclusions if the signed interaction interval excludes zero and the change in dependency difference is scientifically meaningful. The previously registered H222-H233 specificity panel remains important for mapping TP53 and PTEN effects across additional dependencies, so I do not defer it now; these new proposals are registered for subsequent prioritization.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 18 explore. Accepted primary associations remain: TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 (H7), KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 (H13), SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 (H10), MSI-high -> stronger WRN (H8), PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 (H67), CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 (H94). Most are large and validated in the supplied dataset; all remain dataset-specific associations, not causal claims. Active specificity panel H222-H233 remains registered. New follow-ups registered: (1) TP53-loss MDM2 CDKN2A effect restricted to high screen ROC to refine unresolved H100; (2) MYC-amplification MDM2 CDKN2A effect restricted to high screen ROC to refine unresolved H170; (3-8) formal interactions of accepted markers with MYC expression, EMT, ASCL2, SLFN11, PIK3CA mutation, and screen quality. Priority after the H222-H233 panel is to resolve the MDM2 subgroup/screen-quality issues, then accept interactions only if they show material effect modification rather than merely broad robustness. If the active set becomes too large, future exploration should defer lower-priority PTEN-loss specificity tests for unlikely dependencies in favor of high-value refinements. No validation requests remain."
